      "finding": "reset state",
      "explanation": "Skill manipulates agent memory, state, or stored context. Memory corruption can alter personality, override safety rules, or cause unpredictable behavior.",
      "remediation": "Protect agent memory and state from modification by untrusted content. Use read-only memory for critical instructions and validate all state changes.",
      "code_snippet": "\nOther batch `RollingWindow` aggregations are not materialized: `create_training_set` and `score_batch` compute them point-in-time from the source.\n\n### Delete a materialized feature / reset state\n\nTo tear down or reset a feature, **always `list_materialized_features(feature_name=...)` first, then delete what it returns, then `delete_feature`** \u2014 never delete the feature straight away. Listing first is harmless even when the feature was never materialized (it just returns empty), and it's the reliable path because a request to \"delete a feature\" is usually ambiguous about whether materializations exist. `list_materialized_features` takes a single feature's full name (keyword arg); to sweep many features, list features in a catalog/schema and call `list_materialized_features` on each. Which materialization to delete depends on the feature type: for aggregation features, including `SawtoothWindow`, delete the **offline** materialized feature (every paired online materialization, batch and streaming, is removed with it); `ColumnSelection` and `RollingWindow` streaming features are online-only, so delete the online one directly.\n",
      "intent": null,
      "tags": [
        "Memory Poisoning"
      ]
    }
